1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,800
or biggest financial market that has ever existed in the universe by many orders of

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:04,800
magnitude.

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:09,000
It's basically, it's not enough to have 10,000 different financial instruments selling.

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:16,440
There need to be every conceivable instrument selling under every conceivable condition.

5
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:19,240
That could be quadrillions of different financial assets.

6
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:24,080
Unless you've got that, then it still seems like you've got a problem.

7
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:25,880
Is there any other approach?

8
00:00:25,880 --> 00:00:26,880
The answer is yes.

9
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:39,840
There is another approach, very general one, and it's called search theory.

10
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:42,880
Search theory has a few basic assumptions.

11
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:45,920
First, we call the assumption of efforts.

12
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:50,360
If you put more time and energy into searching for what you're looking for, you're more likely

13
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:51,360
to find it.

14
00:00:51,360 --> 00:00:52,360
Simple result.

15
00:00:52,360 --> 00:00:57,640
This could be for socks or gold or a job or a date.

16
00:00:57,640 --> 00:01:02,120
Any of these things, just put in more effort and it raises your odds.

17
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:08,920
Then another assumption, which usually you wouldn't even bother putting into the model,

18
00:01:08,920 --> 00:01:11,840
but you need it to understand the world is just a different searching ability.

19
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:15,440
So like a professional gold miner has more ability to find gold than I do.

20
00:01:15,440 --> 00:01:20,320
You put in a small amount of effort and still it's way more likely to detect it than I am.

21
00:01:20,320 --> 00:01:26,920
And then last, normally search theory assumes rational expectations.

22
00:01:26,920 --> 00:01:32,560
So on average, people are correct in estimating their probability of finding things, their

23
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:36,360
ability to influence those probabilities, and so on.

24
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:41,520
Now the main conclusion that comes out of search theory is people keep searching until

25
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:46,640
the expected marginal benefits of search equals the expected marginal cost.

26
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:51,360
And you can add in a risk aversion adjustment if you want.

27
00:01:51,360 --> 00:01:53,360
That's fine too.

28
00:01:53,360 --> 00:01:54,360
All right.

29
00:01:54,360 --> 00:01:57,400
Now there's lots of different applications to this.

30
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:02,480
In fact, there's almost, we really just talk about this endlessly, so this is my let's

31
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:03,480
say like doing R&D.

32
00:02:03,480 --> 00:02:07,160
So I suppose you want to go and find a cure for cancer.

33
00:02:07,160 --> 00:02:08,160
All right.

34
00:02:08,160 --> 00:02:12,640
Well, first of all, you're more likely to find the cure if you put in some effort.

35
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:13,640
All right.

36
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:15,480
It's a good place to start.

37
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:16,480
Try.

38
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:19,680
You can wait around for the cure to fall into your laps, but it probably will not happen.

39
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:21,880
It's highly unlikely, so put in some effort.

40
00:02:21,880 --> 00:02:22,880
All right.

41
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:24,560
Then secondly, there's a matter of ability.

42
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:29,520
Some people are a lot more likely to cure cancer than I am, such as professional cancer

43
00:02:29,520 --> 00:02:31,240
researchers.

44
00:02:31,240 --> 00:02:35,280
Their odds may be really small, and yet better than my odds.

45
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:38,560
At least they know what cancer is, for example.

46
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:44,800
Whereas I am, oh, it's like your cells, something, like your cells are dividing the wrong way,

47
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:50,400
those people smoking and rats and tar and stuff.

48
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:51,400
All right.

49
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:55,680
Yeah, I wouldn't say I have very little ability to go and explain it.

50
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:57,680
But then finally rational expectations.

51
00:02:57,680 --> 00:03:01,900
So I've got, I think, rational expectations about my ability to cure cancer, which is

52
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:06,360
it's so close to zero, there's no point in me even looking into it.

53
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:12,040
Whereas someone else who knew better, more experienced in training, would think that

54
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:13,040
they would have a higher probability.

55
00:03:13,040 --> 00:03:17,480
Well, of course, these are the people that I talk to who I understand the most say, there's

