00:12:45,480 --> 00:12:48,480
I mean, like even issue by issue, there are some things I've never been worried about forgetting.

240
00:12:48,480 --> 00:12:54,480
Like I wasn't worried that I would forget the due date for my wife's pregnancies.

241
00:12:54,480 --> 00:12:56,480
I didn't bother writing it down.

242
00:12:56,480 --> 00:12:59,480
I heard it, it was very important, and so it was easy to remember.

243
00:12:59,480 --> 00:13:03,480
Whereas other things just go in one year and out the next.

244
00:13:03,480 --> 00:13:05,480
And again, rationalizations.

245
00:13:05,480 --> 00:13:09,480
One thing I will say to myself, I have a very good ability to predict what I'm going to forget.

246
00:13:09,480 --> 00:13:20,480
So 90% of the things my wife says, I want you to remember this, and I'm like, well, either I can get up right now and write it down, or I'm going to forget it.

247
00:13:20,480 --> 00:13:22,480
Those are the two possibilities.

248
00:13:22,480 --> 00:13:27,480
There's no way I'm going to remember to go and call your mom for a feast day.

249
00:13:27,480 --> 00:13:31,480
This is not unlikely, it's going to be astronomically unlikely.

250
00:13:31,480 --> 00:13:38,480
So the least I know what my capabilities are, and that's just not the kind of thing that will stick with me.

251
00:13:38,480 --> 00:13:44,480
One of my favorite heuristics is when someone asks me to order something from a restaurant, I always do their order first.

252
00:13:44,480 --> 00:13:47,480
Because other people's orders just slip right through my head.

253
00:13:47,480 --> 00:13:50,480
And usually I'm like, I can't imagine why anyone would want that food.

254
00:13:50,480 --> 00:13:57,480
So if that is my reaction, well, I better say it immediately before a cub salad.

255
00:13:57,480 --> 00:13:59,480
You want that.

256
00:13:59,480 --> 00:14:02,480
So I'll order that first, because I'm not going to forget what I want.

257
00:14:02,480 --> 00:14:07,480
But I'm very likely to forget what you want.

258
00:14:07,480 --> 00:14:14,480
And then last one, so you also think about this as a story about why some people are funny and other people are not funny.

259
00:14:14,480 --> 00:14:20,480
A standard part of comedy is if you tell a joke too many times, it stops being funny.

260
00:14:20,480 --> 00:14:24,480
So something about comedy is an element of the unexpected.

261
00:14:24,480 --> 00:14:27,480
But not all unexpected things are funny.

262
00:14:27,480 --> 00:14:30,480
In fact, most unexpected things are just boring or annoying.

263
00:14:30,480 --> 00:14:37,480
So you think about a comedian as someone who searches through the vast space of unexpected things to find the small minority of them that are entertaining.

264
00:14:37,480 --> 00:14:40,480
And then they share those things.

265
00:14:40,480 --> 00:14:45,480
And again, effort, ability, and if you're a good comedian, comedic rationalizations.

266
00:14:45,480 --> 00:14:49,480
Although you could also have good rationalizations if you never tell jokes because you realize you're not funny.

267
00:14:49,480 --> 00:14:54,480
That would be another way that you could be totally satisfying the assumptions.

268
00:14:54,480 --> 00:14:59,480
Say, look, no one ever laughs at my jokes, no matter how hard I try, so I'm just not going to try to be funny.

269
00:14:59,480 --> 00:15:04,480
And then people will at least be spared the embarrassment of me not being funny.

270
00:15:04,480 --> 00:15:09,480
Alright, so of course there are many other applications. Yes?

271
00:15:09,480 --> 00:15:14,480
So the searching here doesn't mean searching information?

272
00:15:14,480 --> 00:15:18,480
It's not about searching information?

273
00:15:18,480 --> 00:15:25,480
Well, in a way you could say that certain information is one thing you search for.

274
00:15:25,480 --> 00:15:29,480
Or you could just say I'm searching for information about where the gold is.

275
00:15:29,480 --> 00:15:31,480
Or I'm searching for gold.

276
00:15:31,480 --> 00:15:34,480
Virtually the same thing.

277
00:15:34,480 --> 00:15:42,480
In terms of business, you might say I don't actually find the gold, I search for information about the gold, and then I sell it to the people that physically go there.

278
00:15:42,480 --> 00:15:48,480
So that's a more meaningful way where you can distinguish between searching for the information versus searching for the thing.

279
00:15:48,480 --> 00:15:52,480
I have an algorithm that gives useful predictions about where gold is likely to be found.

280
00:15:52,480 --> 00:15:59,480
I don't personally ever go there, I just sell the information to people who are professional gold finders.

281
00:15:59,480 --> 00:16:07,480
Now, a nice application of search theory is what if there's some people with a very high value of time and so they don't do much searching?

282
00:16:07,480 --> 00:16:12,480
Well, then you'd expect that sellers will actually search for consumers.

283
00:16:12,480 --> 00:16:20,480
So for example, have you ever been a tourist in a place like Istanbul, where for the tourists generally a much higher value of time than the natives for two reasons.

284
00:16:20,480 --> 00:16:29,480
One is they're richer than the natives, but also the tourists may only have 10 hours in Istanbul, whereas the people who live there have their whole lives there.

285
00:16:29,480 --> 00:16:35,480
So someone who's a resident, one more hour of Istanbul is worth two or three dollars to them.

286
00:16:35,480 --> 00:16:39,480
But for the tourists on the other hand, an hour in Istanbul can be worth $200.

287
00:16:39,480 --> 00:16:44,480
You wouldn't even have bothered to get there unless you thought the hours spent there were going to be really good.

288
00:16:44,480 --> 00:16:50,480
Now this happens when some people have a much higher cost of search than others, it makes sense to have specialization.

289
00:16:50,480 --> 00:16:56,480
Where people who are there go and search for you, and then they save you the time.

290
00:16:56,480 --> 00:17:03,480
So in a place like Istanbul, if you look like a tourist, people actually come right up to you on the street and offer to sell you things.

291
00:17:03,480 --> 00:17:09,480
And the result of this is that you have to spend very little time finding souvenirs in Istanbul.

292
00:17:09,480 --> 00:17:14,480
So when the souvenirs just come to you, you walk around and someone will come with a trench coat and open it up.

293
00:17:14,480 --> 00:17:20,480
And it's a perfect fully clothed person with a whole lot of souvenirs.

294
00:17:20,480 --> 00:17:26,480
And I actually found that if they don't have the souvenir you're looking for, they'll just say, wait right here, and they'll run.

295
00:17:26,480 --> 00:17:31,480
And they'll come back and they'll have exactly the souvenir you're looking for.

296
00:17:31,480 --> 00:17:40,480
Now you generally are paying enormous prices compared to what you would pay if you were to go and actually bother to go to a souvenir shop in Hagel.

297
00:17:40,480 --> 00:17:44,480
But this is still an enormous bargain relative to your value of time.

298
00:17:44,480 --> 00:17:50,480
So you might go and spend $10 for a book that actually as a marginal cost only a dollar if you know where to buy the book.

299
00:17:50,480 --> 00:17:55,480
However, as a tourist it might take you 30 minutes to go and find that book.

300
00:17:55,480 --> 00:18:00,480
So then you would be saving $9 by burning up say $100 worth of time.

301
00:18:00,480 --> 00:18:04,480
So this might be your only time you're ever going to be in Istanbul.

302
00:18:04,480 --> 00:18:12,480
And search theory also gives us a very nice story about what it means to be overpaid or underpaid.

303
00:18:12,480 --> 00:18:15,480
Or what it means for there to be a bargain or a rip off.

304
00:18:15,480 --> 00:18:21,480
Because in supply and demand, when someone says something is a bargain or a rip off, a rip off is basically just complaining and whining.

305
00:18:21,480 --> 00:18:28,480
There's no economic meaning to getting a bargain or a rip off if there's a single price that everyone in the market pays.

306
00:18:28,480 --> 00:18:32,480
But with search theory there is a good story.

307
00:18:32,480 --> 00:18:43,480
So basically if someone is overpaid this means that they've found, they've sought out an unusually good deal.

308
00:18:43,480 --> 00:18:48,480
Or again if they're being underpaid you could say they've actually settled for an unusually poor deal.

309
00:18:48,480 --> 00:18:53,480
But again there are reasons for trying both sides of it.

310
00:18:53,480 --> 00:18:58,480
Like you might want to really hold out for a long time to search a lot if the value of your time is low.

311
00:18:58,480 --> 00:19:04,480
In that case you spend a lot of time searching and then when you finally do get an offer it's probably going to be a really good offer.

312
00:19:04,480 --> 00:19:08,480
On the other hand you might be someone who's really new and just feel like look I just don't have time for this.

313
00:19:08,480 --> 00:19:13,480
And then you might just go and settle and just take the very first offer you get even though it's a pretty crummy offer.

314
00:19:13,480 --> 00:19:21,480
Right now in the actual world of employment there's two interesting sides of it.

